This page contains no derivations. It is a methodological note on axiomatics, continuing the reflections of the previous page on how exactly the axioms hold, on existence as consistency, and on Helmholtz versus Lilienthal. The next page restates the same idea in a draft-like way. The argument runs as follows.

(1) One can skip the whole question of deriving or building a science from axioms by postulating a single compact analytic requirement. In geometry: every plane is the zero set of a linear equation a*x + b*y + c*z + d = 0 in the three point coordinates. A line is the common solution set of two such equations. (The interlinear addition says) motions are transformations x -> A x + t leaving a quadratic form invariant, i.e. A^T A = I for x^2 + y^2 + z^2. In modern terms, this is postulating Euclidean space as R^3 with its affine structure and the Euclidean group, i.e. Cartesian analytic geometry with a Klein-style group of motions. Further examples: in mechanics, Hamilton's principle (delta of the integral of L dt = 0); in thermodynamics, the entropy equation (dS = dQ/T, i.e. the existence of an entropy function), which 'contains the whole theory'; in electrodynamics, Maxwell's equations.

(2) Hilbert finds it 'very remarkable' that in all these cases such a short summary of all the axioms is possible. He notes this works only by presupposing arithmetic or analysis. He even suggests that the essence and significance of arithmetic, analysis and differential equations might lie in making such compression possible.

(3) Nevertheless, full insight into the meaning of the axioms comes only from 'parallel passages' with the arithmetical axioms, i.e. from decomposing the compact principle into independent axioms and comparing systems. The next page states the reason explicitly: otherwise one does not see what is contained in the principle, and everything degenerates into the purely formal.

(4) Only then do the fruitful viewpoints appear. In the investigations on the foundations of geometry, the most diverse disciplines prove connected:
- surface theory (surfaces of constant curvature; cf. his 1901 non-existence theorem for complete surfaces of constant negative curvature in R^3);
- quadratic differential forms (Riemann metrics);
- representability as a sum of squares (Grundlagen der Geometrie §§37-38 on ruler-and-gauge constructions and totally positive numbers; 17th problem);
- Cantor's set theory (continuity/completeness axioms);
- group theory (Lie-Helmholtz space problem; Hilbert's 1902 Math. Ann. 56 paper on the foundations of geometry via groups);
- projective versus metric theorems (Desargues, Pascal, the role of the Archimedean axiom).

No computations are made. The note is consistent with Hilbert's program c. 1899-1905: Grundlagen der Geometrie, Paris problem 6, and the 1905 lectures 'Logische Prinzipien des mathematischen Denkens'.
